; ============================================================================
;  .lcl — installer appearance
;
;  electron-builder's stock NSIS wizard is grey Win32 chrome. The app it
;  installs is frameless and near-black. This closes as much of that gap as
;  NSIS actually permits.
;
;  WHAT CANNOT BE THEMED, AND WHY — written down so nobody loses an afternoon
;  looking for the setting:
;
;    The UAC elevation prompt is drawn by Windows itself, on a separate secure
;    desktop no application can reach. Its colours, fonts and wording are fixed.
;    It currently reads "Unknown publisher" because this build carries no
;    code-signing certificate; a certificate replaces that line with the
;    publisher name, and nothing else about that dialog will ever change.
;
;    That is the screen a person judges trustworthiness on, which is why a
;    certificate buys more perceived polish than every pixel below it.
; ============================================================================

!include "MUI2.nsh"

; ---- colours -------------------------------------------------------------
; Straight from app/renderer/styles.css, so the installer cannot drift away
; from the app it installs. NSIS wants 0xRRGGBB.
!define LCL_BG        0x050505     ; --bg
!define LCL_BG_RAISE  0x0B0B0B     ; --bg-raise
!define LCL_TEXT      0xF4F4F5     ; --text
!define LCL_TEXT_DIM  0xA1A1A6     ; --text-dim
!define LCL_LINE      0x2B2B2E     ; --line-strong

!define MUI_BGCOLOR             ${LCL_BG}
!define MUI_TEXTCOLOR           ${LCL_TEXT}
!define MUI_LICENSEPAGE_BGCOLOR ${LCL_BG}

; ---- wording -------------------------------------------------------------
; The stock text talks about "the Setup Wizard" in the third person. This says
; what is actually happening, in the app's own voice.
!define MUI_WELCOMEPAGE_TITLE ".lcl"
!define MUI_WELCOMEPAGE_TEXT \
    "An AI workbench that runs on this machine.$\r$\n$\r$\n\
The models, your files, your knowledge and every tool stay local. Nothing is \
sent anywhere unless you link an endpoint yourself and turn networking on.$\r$\n$\r$\n\
This installs about 1.5 GB. Your sessions and knowledge libraries live in your \
user folder and are never touched by an install or an upgrade."

!define MUI_FINISHPAGE_TITLE "Installed."
!define MUI_FINISHPAGE_TEXT \
    "Open it and pick a model. The load planner chooses the strongest one your \
free memory can actually hold, and gives you the numbers when it cannot.$\r$\n$\r$\n\
Everything runs offline out of the box."
!define MUI_FINISHPAGE_RUN_TEXT "Open .lcl now"

!define MUI_ABORTWARNING_TEXT "Stop installing .lcl?"

; ---- art -----------------------------------------------------------------
; Deliberately NOT defined here.
;
; electron-builder already passes these on the NSIS command line, derived from
; installerSidebar / installerHeader in builder-config.json:
;
;   MUI_WELCOMEFINISHPAGE_BITMAP, MUI_UNWELCOMEFINISHPAGE_BITMAP,
;   MUI_HEADERIMAGE, MUI_HEADERIMAGE_RIGHT, MUI_HEADERIMAGE_BITMAP
;
; Defining them again is a hard "already defined" error that aborts the build —
; which is exactly what the first attempt did. The images are generated by
; devtools/make-installer-art.js into app/assets, the buildResources directory
; this project points at. Writing them to a build/ folder at the repo root
; produced files nothing ever read, and that artefact shipped unbranded.

; ---- the window frame ----------------------------------------------------
; electron-builder's template owns .onGUIInit, so defining it here is a hard
; "Function already exists" error. MUI2 exposes a hook for exactly this case:
; it calls whatever this points at from inside its own GUIINIT, which is the
; first moment $HWNDPARENT exists.
!define MUI_CUSTOMFUNCTION_GUIINIT lclStyleWindow

Function lclStyleWindow
  SetCtlColors $HWNDPARENT ${LCL_TEXT} ${LCL_BG}
FunctionEnd

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT IS AND IS NOT THEMED — measured by building this and looking at it.
;
;   themed   the header strip, its title and subtitle, the window frame, and the
;            welcome and finish pages. MUI paints these from MUI_BGCOLOR and
;            MUI_TEXTCOLOR above; a screenshot of the running installer confirms
;            white on black.
;
;   not      the interior page bodies — install mode, directory, progress. Those
;            are ordinary Win32 dialogs whose controls default to system grey.
;
; Repainting them from the customPageDirectory / customWelcomePage hooks does
; not compile: those macros are inserted at file scope, and FindWindow and
; GetDlgItem are only valid inside a Function or Section. Doing it properly
; needs MUI_PAGE_CUSTOMFUNCTION_SHOW defined before each MUI_PAGE_* macro, and
; electron-builder owns those insertions — so it means replacing their page
; definitions wholesale and owning every future change to their template.
;
; Left alone on purpose. The header, the frame, the art and the words are what a
; person actually reads.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

!macro customPageDirectory
  ; hook kept so a field can be added later without disturbing the page order
!macroend

; ---- close the running app, in EVERY install mode --------------------------
; History, because this broke twice:
;   1. electron-builder's stock CHECK_APP_RUNNING sits inside
;      `${ifNot} ${UAC_IsInnerInstance}` in installSection.nsh — and once
;      machine-wide became the default install, ALL the work happens in the
;      elevated inner instance, so the stock check never ran.
;   2. The first fix defined customCheckAppRunning, which replaces the BODY of
;      that check — but the UAC guard is around the INSERTION, so the custom
;      body was skipped identically. Verified by a user installing over a
;      running app: nothing closed.
; customInit is inserted in .onInit (installer.nsi), which runs in EVERY
; instance, outer and inner, no guard. The second instance to run finds the
; app already gone and does nothing. Close gracefully first so the app can
; stop its own engine; force after; after a force, take llama-server.exe down
; too — a force-killed app cannot clean up its child, and an orphaned engine
; keeps resources\runtimes locked.
; WHY $SYSDIR AND NOT %SYSTEMROOT% — measured, after being wrong twice.
;
; electron-builder's own template (and the first two versions of this macro)
; invoke `%SYSTEMROOT%\System32\cmd.exe`. NSIS does not expand environment
; variables inside a string, and CreateProcess will not resolve a literal
; `%SYSTEMROOT%\...` path, so nsExec::Exec cannot launch it at all. It pushes
; the string "error" — never an exit code — so `${if} $R0 == 0` is false and
; THE ENTIRE CLOSE BLOCK IS SKIPPED. That is why every previous attempt
; "worked" in review and did nothing on the machine.
;
; Compiled and run as a probe on the target machine:
;     %SYSTEMROOT%\System32\cmd.exe form  -> [error]   (never ran)
;     "$SYSDIR\taskkill.exe" form         -> [128]     (ran; not-found)
;
; $SYSDIR is NSIS's own resolved system directory, so it is a real path at
; compile-and-run time. taskkill's exit code is also the only detection
; needed — 0 means it was running and has been signalled, 128 means it was
; not — which removes the tasklist pipe, find.exe, and their quoting entirely.
; NO CONDITIONALS. Every previous version of this macro branched on an exit
; code, and every one of those branches was wrong on the real machine:
;
;   %SYSTEMROOT%\System32\cmd.exe   -> nsExec returns "error", never a code,
;                                      because NSIS does not expand the
;                                      variable and CreateProcess will not
;                                      resolve the literal path. Block skipped.
;   taskkill /im (graceful)         -> returns 1, not 0, when ANY matched
;                                      process has no window to close — and
;                                      .lcl is five processes, four of them
;                                      windowless helpers. Block skipped.
;
; Both measured by compiling this exact macro and running it against a live
; process. So the sequence is now unconditional: ask nicely, wait, insist.
; Against a stopped app all three calls return 128 and do nothing, which is
; the correct behaviour for "not running" — no detection required to get it.
!macro LCL_CLOSE_RUNNING_APP
  DetailPrint `Closing "${PRODUCT_NAME}" if it is running...`
  ; ask: a WM_CLOSE lets the app save its sessions and stop its engine
  nsExec::Exec `"$SYSDIR\taskkill.exe" /im "${APP_EXECUTABLE_FILENAME}"`
  Pop $R0
  Sleep 3000
  ; insist: anything still holding a file after the grace period goes down,
  ; and the engine child with it — a force-killed app cannot stop
  ; llama-server itself, and an orphan keeps resources\runtimes locked
  nsExec::Exec `"$SYSDIR\taskkill.exe" /f /im "${APP_EXECUTABLE_FILENAME}"`
  Pop $R1
  nsExec::Exec `"$SYSDIR\taskkill.exe" /f /im "llama-server.exe"`
  Pop $R2
  Sleep 1500
!macroend

!macro customInit
  !insertmacro LCL_CLOSE_RUNNING_APP
!macroend

; Kept as well: covers the per-user path through the template's own flow.
; Finds nothing when customInit already closed the app.
!macro customCheckAppRunning
  !insertmacro LCL_CLOSE_RUNNING_APP
!macroend

!macro customInstall
  ; A first run has no settings file and the app's own defaults are correct, so
  ; there is deliberately nothing seeded here. Writing preferences from an
  ; installer means an upgrade can silently overwrite a choice the user made.
!macroend

!macro customUnInstall
  ; The user's data directory is NOT removed. Sessions, knowledge libraries and
  ; the encrypted endpoint store live there, and an uninstall that destroys a
  ; year of someone's work because they wanted to move the app is indefensible.
  ; deleteAppDataOnUninstall stays false in builder-config.json for the same
  ; reason.
!macroend
